    <item>
      <title>Join Us</title>
      <link>https://bionicvisionlab.org/join/</link>
      <pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate>
      
      <guid>https://bionicvisionlab.org/join/</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;We welcome enthusiastic, computationally minded people interested in understanding vision and advancing sight restoration for people living with blindness. We especially value people who know what they want to learn, take initiative, and follow projects through to completion.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Before reaching out, please check our &lt;a href=&#34;https://docs.google.com/document/d/1Y1wzFVdp-FCoGM47okaW5eYdOOfpgXD5nM9Q7DpwAMo/edit?usp=sharing&#34;&gt;Lab Manual&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2 class=&#34;section-title fill-width&#34;&gt;PhD Position: Machine Learning for Visual Neuroprostheses (Fall 2027)&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;We are recruiting one PhD student through the &lt;a href=&#34;https://cs.ucsb.edu/education/graduate/how-to-apply&#34; target=&#34;_blank&#34;&gt;UCSB Department of Computer Science&lt;/a&gt; to join the lab in Fall &amp;lsquo;27.
Applications are due December 16, 2026.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The project focuses on computational models and machine-learning methods for visual cortical prostheses. This includes models that predict &lt;a href=&#34;../publications/2026-02-fuzzing-the-brain/&#34;&gt;safe neural activity&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href=&#34;../publications/2022-12-brain-like-phosphene-model/&#34;&gt;visual percepts&lt;/a&gt;, and &lt;a href=&#34;../publications/2026-09-percept-aware-surgical-planning/&#34;&gt;where to place electrodes&lt;/a&gt;, as well as &lt;a href=&#34;../publications/2026-08-deep-neural-activity-shaping&#34;&gt;inverse/control methods&lt;/a&gt; for producing desired percepts.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;We are looking for someone with a strong ML background, programming skills beyond vibe coding, and a desire to work closely with real neurotechnology and industry teams developing next-generation brain-computer interfaces.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Prior neuroscience experience is useful, but not required.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2 class=&#34;section-title fill-width&#34;&gt;Potential PhD Position: Low Vision &amp; Computational Psychophysics (Fall 2027)&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;We may also recruit one PhD student through the &lt;a href=&#34;https://psych.ucsb.edu/prospective-students-0&#34; target=&#34;_blank&#34;&gt;UCSB Department of Psychological &amp;amp; Brain Sciences&lt;/a&gt; for Fall &amp;lsquo;27, contingent on funding and departmental approval. Applications are due December 1, 2026.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The project would focus on understanding how vision loss changes visual behavior and using quantitative models to predict how people adapt to impaired or restored vision. Possible directions include central and peripheral vision loss, patient-specific modeling, eye movements and active vision, gaze-contingent simulation, and XR-based psychophysics.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;We are particularly interested in candidates with a strong quantitative background who want to combine psychophysics, computational modeling, and vision science. Prior experience with low vision or clinical populations is useful, but not required.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Because this position is not yet guaranteed, prospective applicants with a particularly strong fit are encouraged to contact us before applying.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;div class=&#34;callout-note&#34;&gt;
  &lt;i class=&#34;fas fa-info-circle fa-lg&#34; aria-hidden=&#34;true&#34;&gt;&lt;/i&gt;
  &lt;div class=&#34;small&#34;&gt;Because we receive a large volume of prospective-student inquiries, we are unable to respond to every individual message. We do read inquiries from prospective PhD students, but generally respond only when there appears to be an especially strong match with our current openings and research directions. Lack of a response should not be interpreted as discouragement from applying through the normal UCSB admissions process.&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;h2 class=&#34;section-title fill-width&#34;&gt;Prospective Postdocs&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;We do not currently have a funded postdoctoral opening, but we are interested in hearing from strong candidates whose research aligns closely with the lab. Areas of particular interest include computational neuroprosthetics and NeuroAI, including models of artificial vision across the retina, LGN, and visual cortex; neural population modeling, digital twins, and closed-loop control; and vision science focused on low vision, central and peripheral vision loss, active visual behavior, and XR-based approaches to studying or augmenting vision.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;If you expect to graduate within the next year and see a strong fit with our work, please reach out early so we can discuss potential projects and explore fellowship opportunities together.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2 class=&#34;section-title fill-width&#34;&gt;Junior Lab Trainees&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;For UCSB undergraduates, the Junior Lab Trainee program is the main entry point into the lab. Trainees attend bi-weekly paper discussions and participate reliably in ongoing behavioral studies (XR, psychophysics, and so on).&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Successful completion of a quarter as a Junior Trainee is often a prerequisite for becoming a Senior Research Assistant, although promotion is not guaranteed.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;We accept a limited cohort each quarter. To be considered, please &lt;a href=&#34;https://forms.gle/XGuijmyZX9LSedwn6&#34;&gt;join our waitlist&lt;/a&gt;.
If you already have specialized research experience, you may indicate interest in a Senior RA role on the form.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2 class=&#34;section-title fill-width&#34;&gt;All Other Positions&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;We are currently at capacity for Senior RAs and Master&amp;rsquo;s students, and cannot accommodate summer interns, high school students, or remote projects.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;small class=&#34;mt-5&#34;&gt;This page was last updated on September 22, 2026.&lt;/small&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;!--
&lt;div class=&#34;fill-width small mt-0 mb-4&#34;&gt;
  &lt;p class=&#34;mb-0&#34;&gt;
    &lt;i class=&#34;fas fa-exclamation-triangle&#34; aria-hidden=&#34;true&#34;&gt;&lt;/i&gt;
    We receive many applications each year. To help us give fair consideration to all candidates:
  &lt;/p&gt;
  &lt;ul class=&#34;mt-0 pt-0 mb-2&#34;&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;Please do not send generic or copy-and-paste emails. Applications must show genuine familiarity with our work.&lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Do not use AI/LLM-generated statements.&lt;/strong&gt; As an AI lab, we can tell the difference, and it will not make a good impression.&lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;There is no need to send multiple follow-up emails.&lt;/li&gt;
  &lt;/ul&gt;
  &lt;p class=&#34;mb-0&#34;&gt;Messages that do not follow these guidelines will not receive a response. Thank you for your understanding.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;h2 class=&#34;section-title fill-width&#34;&gt;Postdoc in Visual/Computational Neuroscience (NeuroAI)&lt;/h2&gt;
We are excited to invite a self-driven and enthusiastic postdoctoral researcher to our &lt;a href=&#34;https://bionicvisionlab.org/research/active-vision-sensorimotor/&#34;&gt;VisNav&lt;/a&gt; team.
This NeuroAI project, at the exciting crossroads of visual and computational neuroscience, is part of the NIH BRAIN Initiative that seeks to elucidate how the mouse brain processes visual information during active exploration to support visual navigation. Our recent &lt;a href=&#34;https://bionicvisionlab.org/publications/2023-12-v1-mouse/&#34;&gt;NeurIPS paper&lt;/a&gt; highlights how a multimodal recurrent net can integrate gaze-contingent visual input with behavioral and temporal dynamics to explain V1 activity in freely moving mice. We are now seeking to extend this work to higher-order visual areas (HVAs) in collaboration with the labs of Spencer Smith (UCSB), Michael Goard (UCSB), and Cris Niell (University of Oregon).
What we offer:
-	A welcoming, inclusive, and collaborative environment that combines expertise in neuroscience, computer science, and cognitive sciences.
-	A fully funded, unionized position, offering a competitive salary and excellent benefits, for a minimum 2-year commitment.
-	An opportunity to work with diverse experts and engage with unique neural activity data sets, collected using state-of-the-art techniques.
We are looking for:
-	A passionate individual with a solid foundation in visual and computational neuroscience.
-	A PhD in (computational) neuroscience, computer science, cognitive sciences, statistics, or a related field
-	Strong communication and teamwork skills, with proficiency in programming and statistical analysis
To apply, please email your CV and contacts for 2-3 references to &lt;a href=&#34;mailto:mbeyeler&amp;#64;ucsb&amp;#46;edu&#34;&gt;Michael&lt;/a&gt;.
&lt;h2 class=&#34;section-title fill-width&#34;&gt;Junior RA Positions&lt;/h2&gt;
We are currently looking for junior research assistants (&lt;a href=&#34;https://docs.google.com/document/d/1Y1wzFVdp-FCoGM47okaW5eYdOOfpgXD5nM9Q7DpwAMo/edit#heading=h.b7fsq3mt32v0&#34; target=&#34;_blank&#34;&gt;Junior RAs&lt;/a&gt;). As a Junior RA, your responsibilities are three-fold:
- complete readings assigned by the lab manager/your grad student mentor; afterwards, meet with them to discuss
- attend project meetings/training sessions as your schedule allows (no need to attend lab meetings)
- participate in one or more of our ongoing behavioral studies
This is a great way to get introduced to the lab&#39;s work and the field of bionic vision.
In the future, this may lead to a Senior RA position where you can contribute more substantially to our research.
Junior RAs typically earn course credit via PSY-99/199 (P/NP or letter grade), CS-196, or your department&#39;s equivalent of Directed Research.
For more details, see our &lt;a href=&#34;https://docs.google.com/document/d/1Y1wzFVdp-FCoGM47okaW5eYdOOfpgXD5nM9Q7DpwAMo/edit?usp=sharing&#34;&gt;Lab Manual&lt;/a&gt;.
If you are interested in becoming a Junior RA, please reach out to our &lt;a href=&#34;https://bionicvisionlab.org/people&#34;&gt;Lab Manager&lt;/a&gt;.
--&gt;
&lt;!--
In the future, and more generally, we often accept students through the following competitive programs:
- In Computer Science (CS): &lt;a href=&#34;https://cs.ucsb.edu/education/undergraduate/special-programs&#34; target=&#34;_blank&#34;&gt;Distinction in the Major Program (DIMAP)&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href=&#34;https://ersp.cs.ucsb.edu/&#34; target=&#34;_blank&#34;&gt;Early Research Scholars Program (ERSP)&lt;/a&gt; 
- In Psychological &amp; Brain Sciences (PBS): &lt;a href=&#34;https://psych.ucsb.edu/undergraduate/honors-program&#34; target=&#34;_blank&#34;&gt;PBS Honors Program&lt;/a&gt;
- In the College of Engineering (CoE): &lt;a href=&#34;https://engineering.ucsb.edu/undergraduate/college-engineering-honors-program&#34; target=&#34;_blank&#34;&gt;CoE Honors Program&lt;/a&gt;
- Across campus: &lt;a href=&#34;https://cbsr.ucsb.edu/seeds&#34; target=&#34;_blank&#34;&gt;Student Engagement and Enrichment in Data Science (SEEDS)&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href=&#34;https://blacksinneurocomp.com/&#34; target=&#34;_blank&#34;&gt;Black Researchers Advancing Neuroscience (BRAIN)&lt;/a&gt;
Summer programs:
- for high school students: &lt;a href=&#34;https://www.summer.ucsb.edu/programs/research-mentorship-program/overview&#34; target=&#34;_blank&#34;&gt;Research Mentorship Program (RMP)&lt;/a&gt;
- for undergrad students: &lt;a href=&#34;https://www.ucop.edu/graduate-studies/initiatives-outreach/uc-leads.html&#34; target=&#34;_blank&#34;&gt;UC Leadership Excellence through Advanced DegreeS (LEADS)&lt;/a&gt;
--&gt;
&lt;!--
&lt;h2 class=&#34;section-title fill-width&#34;&gt;PhD Positions for Fall 2024&lt;/h2&gt;
We have a limited number of opportunities to join us as a PhD student in Fall 2024. The deadline is December 2023.
We are especially looking for (but not limited to):
1. &lt;a href=&#34;https://cs.ucsb.edu&#34; target=&#34;_blank&#34;&gt;CS&lt;/a&gt;/&lt;a href=&#34;https://ece.ucsb.edu&#34; target=&#34;_blank&#34;&gt;ECE&lt;/a&gt;: Computer vision (CV) / human-computer interaction (HCI) students interested in &lt;a href=&#34;https://bionicvisionlab.org/research/assistive-vision-navigation/&#34;&gt;assistive technologies&lt;/a&gt; and/or &lt;a href=&#34;https://bionicvisionlab.org/research/active-vision-sensorimotor/&#34;&gt;Edge AI&lt;/a&gt;. You may develop novel scene understanding algorithms, event-based CV systems, and/or prototypes of near-future accessibility aids. Your goal will be to publish at top-tier venues such as CVPR, ASSETS, or CHI.
2. &lt;a href=&#34;https://psych.ucsb.edu&#34; target=&#34;_blank&#34;&gt;PBS&lt;/a&gt;: Vision science students interested in working with real &lt;a href=&#34;https://bionicvisionlab.org/research/vision-loss-restoration-adaptation/&#34;&gt;low vision&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href=&#34;https://bionicvisionlab.org/research/artificial-vision-pathway/&#34;&gt;bionic eye patients&lt;/a&gt;. You may run behavioral studies and/or develop computational models to further our understanding of the neural code of vision in health and disease. You don&#39;t need a CS background for this, but should have some experience with vision/cognition, psychophysics, and/or clinical populations.
Please apply through the &lt;a href=&#34;https://www.graddiv.ucsb.edu/how-apply/application-and-admission-checklist&#34; target=&#34;_blank&#34;&gt;Grad Admissions Portal&lt;/a&gt;.
You will then be able to indicate your wish to work with us in the &#34;Major and Degree Objective&#34; tab under &#34;Faculty Interests&#34;. 
US citizens and permanent residents may apply for a &lt;a href=&#34;https://www.graddiv.ucsb.edu/how-apply/faqs-applicants#Graduate-Fee&#34;&gt;fee waiver&lt;/a&gt;.
&lt;small&gt;&lt;i class=&#34;fas fa-info-circle&#34; aria-hidden=&#34;true&#34;&gt;&lt;/i&gt;
Please note that we get a lot of emails from prospective PhD students, so Michael will not be able to respond to messages that aren&#39;t clearly tailored to the lab&#39;s vision, especially if it is apparent that the same &#34;boilerplate&#34; message is being sent to many different labs.
You can make your application stand out by demonstrating that you have spent time on our website, &lt;i&gt;seen this message&lt;/i&gt;, and thought hard about why our lab is a good fit for your skills and interests.&lt;/small&gt;
&lt;h2 class=&#34;section-title fill-width&#34;&gt;All Other Positions&lt;/h2&gt;
We are completely full. Undergraduate students, please check back in January 2024 for Winter Quarter positions.
&lt;h2 class=&#34;section-title&#34;&gt;Postdocs&lt;/h2&gt;
Please &lt;a href=&#34;../people/beyeler_michael&#34;&gt;contact Michael&lt;/a&gt; with your CV and a brief statement
of research accomplishments, interests, and career plans.
Although all applicants are welcome, we are especially looking for expertise at the intersection of computational neuroscience and data science. 
To be considered, you will need at least one publication 
in a high-quality international conference (computer science) 
or journal (neuroscience) 
and you need to have a reasonable chance of getting a fellowship
to support your stay at UCSB.
&lt;h2 class=&#34;section-title fill-width&#34;&gt;CS/ECE Master&#39;s Students&lt;/h2&gt;
We have several MS positions available for Fall 2021:
We are looking for students interested in applying their methodological skills to research problems in bionic vision. 
- Human-computer interaction: Build VR/AR/XR applications applied to low vision and bionic vision (Unity, compute shaders, image processing, bionic vision simulations, eye tracking)
- Biomedical image analysis: Build predictive models applied to biomedical image datasets (registration, segmentation, self-supervised learning)- 
 - Computer vision: Build predictive models applied to biomedical image datasets (registration, segmentation,
  retinal fundus imaging, optical coherence tomography). 
 - Machine learning/data science: Build predictive models applied to real-world datasets collected on retinal prosthesis
  patients (classification, regression, time-series analysis, interpretable models, heterogeneous data). 
- Neuromorphic engineering: Build brain-inspired event-based vision systems applied to scene understanding (silicon retina, spiking neural networks)
 - Software engineering/parallel programming: Develop parallelization back ends for
  &lt;a href=&#34;https://githb.com/pulse2percept/pulse2percept&#34;&gt;pulse2percept&lt;/a&gt;, our open-source Python-based simulation 
  framework (Python, Cython, SciPy, OpenMP, GPGPU, JAX).
Please &lt;a href=&#34;../people/beyeler_michael&#34;&gt;contact Michael&lt;/a&gt; to set up a time to meet.
Typical responsibilities include one or more of the following:
- Assist in running behavioral studies (typically PSY-99 / 199)
- Develop applications for &lt;a href=&#34;https://githb.com/pulse2percept/pulse2percept&#34;&gt;pulse2percept&lt;/a&gt; (typically CS-196)
- Get hands-on research experience by shadowing a PhD student
- Process and analyze scientific data
- Perform literature reviews
- Attend weekly lab meetings and present once a quarter
&lt;h2 class=&#34;section-title fill-width&#34;&gt;Undergraduate Students&lt;/h2&gt;
In addition, we often accept students through the following competitive programs:
- In Computer Science (CS): &lt;a href=&#34;https://cs.ucsb.edu/education/undergraduate/special-programs&#34; target=&#34;_blank&#34;&gt;Distinction in the Major Program (DIMAP)&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href=&#34;https://ersp.cs.ucsb.edu/&#34; target=&#34;_blank&#34;&gt;Early Research Scholars Program (ERSP)&lt;/a&gt; 
- In Psychological &amp; Brain Sciences (PBS): &lt;a href=&#34;https://psych.ucsb.edu/undergraduate/honors-program&#34; target=&#34;_blank&#34;&gt;PBS Honors Program&lt;/a&gt;
- In the College of Engineering (CoE): &lt;a href=&#34;https://engineering.ucsb.edu/undergraduate/college-engineering-honors-program&#34; target=&#34;_blank&#34;&gt;CoE Honors Program&lt;/a&gt;
- Across campus: &lt;a href=&#34;https://cbsr.ucsb.edu/seeds&#34; target=&#34;_blank&#34;&gt;Student Engagement and Enrichment in Data Science (SEEDS)&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href=&#34;https://blacksinneurocomp.com/&#34; target=&#34;_blank&#34;&gt;Black Researchers Advancing Neuroscience (BRAIN)&lt;/a&gt;
--&gt;
&lt;hr class=&#34;section-rule&#34;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
&lt;div class=&#34;gallery2 caption-position-none caption-effect-slide hover-effect-zoom hover-transition&#34; itemscope itemtype=&#34;http://schema.org/ImageGallery&#34;&gt;
  &lt;div class=&#34;box&#34;&gt;
    &lt;figure itemprop=&#34;associatedMedia&#34; itemscope itemtype=&#34;http://schema.org/ImageObject&#34;&gt;
      &lt;div class=&#34;img&#34; style=&#34;background-image: url(&#39;/img/gallery/001%20At%20Fall%20Vision%20Meeting%20with%20Dan%20Adams%20Eduardo%20Fernandez%20Xing%20Chen%20Frederik%20Ceyssens%20Antonio%20Lozano%20Jan%20Antolik.jpg&#39;);&#34;&gt;
        &lt;img itemprop=&#34;thumbnail&#34; src=&#34;https://bionicvisionlab.org/img/gallery/001%20At%20Fall%20Vision%20Meeting%20with%20Dan%20Adams%20Eduardo%20Fernandez%20Xing%20Chen%20Frederik%20Ceyssens%20Antonio%20Lozano%20Jan%20Antolik.jpg&#34; alt=&#34;Selfie of seven researchers wearing conference name badges, standing in front of rows of red auditorium seats. From left to right, back to front: Dan Adams, Eduardo Fernandez, Xing Chen, Frederik Ceyssens, Michael Beyeler, Jan Antolik, and Antonio Lozano.&#34;/&gt;
